{"created": "2026-10-03T08:07:49.874413+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-03T08:07:50.186281+00:00", "id": 23117392, "conceptrecid": "22128975", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23117392", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.22128975", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23117392", "metadata": {"title": "SeraEdit: Scoped and Validated Language-Guided MusicXML Editing (SoftwareX R1 source and evidence)", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23117392", "publication_date": "2026-10-03", "description": "<p>SeraEdit is local-first research software for scoped, source-bound MusicXML editing through ScorePatch transactions, deterministic validation and reversible execution. This SoftwareX R1 revision corrects fixture reproducibility and source-archive completeness, and includes saved DeepSeek three-condition outputs, a 200-case constructed-error study, and native MuseScore bridge evidence.</p>\n<p>On the development benchmark, strict editing success is 72/110 for Full Rewrite, 65/110 for Patch Only and 63/110 for Sera Full. Sera Full records no measured non-target changes among 73 output scores, but erroneously refuses 34 executable requests. These results demonstrate a protection/completion tradeoff, not overall superiority or held-out generalization. The 720-run rule-based replay is regression evidence on the same benchmark.</p>\n<p>Hosted CI passed 447 Python tests from an isolated source archive, 133 frontend tests, the production build, and 14 saved-live-evidence checks. The uploaded archives retain the exact tested bytes. Detailed commit provenance and verification reports are included.</p>\n<p>This deposit contains source and research evidence, with no new desktop installer. The manuscript snapshot represents the tested preparation stage; its pending publication fields do not describe the final journal submission. The original version remains at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22128976.</p>\n<p>Code is MIT licensed; the synthetic benchmark is CC0-1.0. Dependencies retain their own licenses.</p>